{"name":"Deep Mooring","hex":"#3d4c46","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"deep","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Extending, reaching or positioned far from a point of reference, especially downwards.\nExtending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively.","Extending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Far, especially far down through something or into something, physically or figuratively.","In a profound, not superficial, manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/deep"},{"term":"mooring","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of moor"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A place to moor a vessel.","The act of securing a vessel with a cable or anchor etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mooring"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mooring","blocks":[{"text":"A mooring is any permanent structure to which a seaborne vessel may be secured. Examples include quays, wharfs, jetties, piers, anchor buoys, and mooring buoys. A ship is secured to a mooring to forestall free movement of the ship on the water. An 'anchor mooring' fixes a vessel's position relative to a point on the bottom of a waterway without connecting the vessel to shore. As a verb, mooring refers to the act of attaching a vessel to a mooring."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Mooring_Post%2C_Eisenhower_Pier_-_geograph.org.uk_-_552845.jpg/330px-Mooring_Post%2C_Eisenhower_Pier_-_geograph.org.uk_-_552845.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mooring","wikidata":"Q138218756"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Mooring.jpg/500px-Mooring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm